WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.070
Four in the afternoon.

2
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.420
A supervisor gets a request: send updated guidance

3
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.400
to every case manager by end of day.

4
00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.300
The guidance changes how a form is filled

5
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.930
out, and the supervisor hasn't read it closely.

6
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.951
First question: who set the end of day,

7
00:00:15.951 --> 00:00:18.182
and what happens if it goes out tomorrow

8
00:00:18.182 --> 00:00:18.740
morning instead?

9
00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.030
Sometimes the answer is, a law says so.

10
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.987
Often it's, someone just said by end of

11
00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:25.320
day.

12
00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.190
And that difference matters.

13
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.316
Research on decisions under time pressure finds people

14
00:00:30.316 --> 00:00:31.320
shift to shortcuts.

15
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.758
They look at less information and lean on

16
00:00:33.758 --> 00:00:34.270
what's quick.

17
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:35.540
Fine for low stakes.

18
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.310
Risky for guidance a whole division will follow.

19
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:39.420
Here's another.

20
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.834
The monthly report shows four hundred cases closed,

21
00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:43.570
a record.

22
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.960
The program manager is preparing remarks.

23
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.650
Celebrate it, surely.

24
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.160
People worked hard.

25
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.570
Celebrate the effort, yes.

26
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.267
But ask what four hundred closures means for

27
00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:55.560
people.

28
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.487
How many closed because services started, and how

29
00:00:58.487 --> 00:01:00.330
many because a form wasn't returned?

30
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.600
Ah.

31
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:04.190
A closed case isn't the same as a

32
00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.210
family that got help.

33
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:06.940
Output is what we produce.

34
00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.210
Outcomes are what changes for people.

35
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.749
Under pressure, output is easier to count, so

36
00:01:11.749 --> 00:01:13.080
it quietly becomes the goal.

37
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:15.452
So what do you actually do in the

38
00:01:15.452 --> 00:01:15.630
moment?

39
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.158
End of day, a case manager is about

40
00:01:18.158 --> 00:01:20.336
to close a case because a form never

41
00:01:20.336 --> 00:01:20.880
came back.

42
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.550
A short pause routine.

43
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.038
Three questions: who does this affect, what would

44
00:01:25.038 --> 00:01:27.176
I need to know to be confident, and

45
00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:28.780
is anything about this situation unusual?

46
00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.211
And in this case a colleague mentions the

47
00:01:32.211 --> 00:01:34.832
household's preferred language isn't English, and the reminder

48
00:01:34.832 --> 00:01:36.470
went out in English only.

49
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.740
Then the pause just did its job.

50
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.187
The right next step is a reminder they

51
00:01:41.187 --> 00:01:42.510
can read, not a closure.

52
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.880
But some deadlines can't move.

53
00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.763
A new eligibility process launches on a date

54
00:01:47.763 --> 00:01:50.257
set by law, and notices in Somali and

55
00:01:50.257 --> 00:01:52.750
Hmong won't be ready for two more weeks.

56
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.500
Then you protect quality another way.

57
00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.003
A quick premortem helps: imagine it's a month

58
00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:00.297
after launch and it went badly for families

59
00:02:00.297 --> 00:02:01.730
who read Somali or Hmong.

60
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:02.600
What happened?

61
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.790
They never heard about the change.

62
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.020
Or they heard and didn't know what to

63
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.240
do.

64
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.190
So you plan for that now: community partners,

65
00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.590
interpreters on the phone line, extra time to

66
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:12.890
respond.

67
00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.060
The date holds.

68
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.730
The harm doesn't have to.

69
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.904
That's the whole course: tell a true deadline

70
00:02:18.904 --> 00:02:21.429
from a habit, and protect the decisions that

71
00:02:21.429 --> 00:02:22.060
matter most.
